From: =?UTF-8?q?Sergi=E3=83=BB=E3=82=BB=E3=83=AB=E3=82=B8?=
 <xerpi.g.12@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 16:51:52 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 17/52] kernel: fix some sync primitive errors and edge cases
 found on hardware (#4224)

* kernel: keep a fired timer event when setting a new one

This reverts commit 54cbfd540. On a Vita, sceKernelSetTimerEvent leaves
an event the timer already reported in place, so a poll right after it
still sees it.

* kernel: write the result of a failed simple event poll or wait

A Vita writes 0 to the result pattern of a failed poll or a timed out
wait, and the error event, 0x10, to that of a wait a delete or a cancel
ends. Vita3K left the result untouched.

* kernel: check the name, priority and stack size of a new thread

A Vita refuses to create a thread with a NULL name (0x80022005), a
priority outside 0x40 to 0xBF or 0x100000E0 to 0x1000011F
(ILLEGAL_PRIORITY), or a stack size of 0 or less (ILLEGAL_STACK_SIZE).
Vita3K crashed on the NULL name and accepted the rest.

* kernel: give a thread created with priority 0 its creator's priority

Priority 0 stands for the current priority, but a thread created with it
got priority 0 itself, outside the range game threads may use. On a Vita
it starts at the creating thread's current priority, so do that.

* kernel: check semaphore counts and attributes

- Creating a semaphore with a negative count, a maximum of 0 or less or
  a count above the maximum gives ILLEGAL_COUNT, and attributes other
  than the priority and openable ones give ILLEGAL_ATTR. A Vita refuses
  these, Vita3K created the semaphore.
- A signal of a negative count and a poll or wait for 0, a negative
  count or more than the maximum give ILLEGAL_COUNT, as on a Vita.
  Vita3K asserted on a negative poll and waited forever for more than
  the maximum.

* kernel: time out waits for a thread's end, and refuse waiting for one's own

sceKernelWaitThreadEnd ignored its timeout and waited forever, and a
thread waiting for its own end hung. Like on a Vita, the wait now ends
with WAIT_TIMEOUT and writes the time left back, and a thread waiting
for its own end gets ILLEGAL_THREAD_ID.

* kernel: time out timer waits

A wait on a timer ignored its timeout, so with no event set it waited
forever. It now ends with WAIT_TIMEOUT when its timeout runs out before
an event, and writes the time left back, as on a Vita.

* kernel: take the mutex back after a condition wait times out

A condition variable wait that timed out returned without its mutex,
so the caller's unlock that follows failed. On a Vita it owns the mutex
again, so the wait now takes it back before returning WAIT_TIMEOUT.

* kernel: check mutex counts, owners and attributes

- A lock, try lock or unlock count of 0 or less, or above 1 on a mutex
  that isn't recursive, gives ILLEGAL_COUNT.
- Unlocking a mutex the caller doesn't own gives NOT_OWNED, and
  unlocking more than is locked gives UNLOCK_UDF, with the heavy or
  lightweight mutex code. Vita3K returned success or the lightweight
  code for both.
- Locking a heavy mutex past 0x7FFFFFFF gives LOCK_OVF. A lightweight
  mutex's count just wraps, as on a Vita.
- Creating a mutex with a count above 1 needs the recursive attribute,
  where the check was the wrong way around, and attributes other than
  recursive, ceiling, priority and openable give ILLEGAL_ATTR.

* kernel: check event flag patterns, wait modes and attributes

A Vita refuses a poll or wait for pattern 0 with INVALID_ARGUMENT, wait
modes with unknown bits or both clear modes with ILLEGAL_MODE, and
attributes other than multi, priority and openable with ILLEGAL_ATTR.
Vita3K treated pattern 0 as a condition that's never met and took the
rest.

* kernel: check simple event patterns and attributes

Setting, pulsing or clearing bits outside the 8 user bits now fails
with ILLEGAL_PATTERN, as does creating an event with them set. Waiting
or polling for no bits fails with INVALID_ARGUMENT, and creating one
with unknown attributes fails with ILLEGAL_ATTR.

* kernel: report the create and delete events of simple events

A new simple event starts with its create event set, and a wait for its
delete event ends with that event when the event is deleted, instead of
with an error. Polls and waits now get only the bits they waited for,
not every bit of the event.

* kernel: check message pipe types, sizes, attributes and wait modes

Creating a message pipe that isn't of the user main type now fails
with ILLEGAL_TYPE, one with a size of 0 with ILLEGAL_SIZE, and one with
attributes it doesn't take, like the thread priority attribute of the
other primitives, with ILLEGAL_ATTR. Sending or receiving with unknown
wait mode bits fails with ILLEGAL_MODE.

* kernel: fail ASAP message pipe transfers of 0 bytes

An ASAP send or receive must move at least 1 byte, so one of 0 bytes
can't be done, and a try send or receive of 0 bytes now fails with
MSG_PIPE_FULL or MSG_PIPE_EMPTY. A failed try send or receive also
reports 0 bytes now.

* kernel: check reader/writer lock attributes and which lock is held

A thread holding the write lock can't take the read lock too, and one
holding a read lock can't take the write lock, both fail with
FAILED_TO_LOCK. Write unlocking a lock the thread doesn't hold for
writing fails with NOT_OWNED, read unlocking one with no readers with
UNLOCK_UDF, and read unlocking one the thread doesn't hold for reading
with FAILED_TO_UNLOCK. Creating a lock with unknown attributes fails
with ILLEGAL_ATTR.

* kernel: check the target of a condition signal

Signaling a condition to a thread that doesn't exist now fails with
UNKNOWN_THREAD_ID, and to one that doesn't wait on the condition with
ILLEGAL_THREAD_ID, instead of logging and succeeding.

* kernel: end a condition wait whose mutex is deleted with WAIT_DELETE_MUTEX

A condition wait whose mutex is deleted before the wait takes it back
now ends with WAIT_DELETE_MUTEX, or WAIT_DELETE_LW_MUTEX for a
lightweight one, instead of the unknown mutex error.

* kernel: refuse creating a thread with no entry

Creating a thread with a NULL entry now fails with ILLEGAL_ADDR,
instead of making a thread that crashes when it starts.

* kernel: check timer event types and intervals

Setting a timer event of a type that is neither set nor pulse now fails
with ILLEGAL_TYPE, and one without an interval with ILLEGAL_ADDR
instead of INVALID_ARGUMENT.

* kernel: return all ones for the time of an unknown timer

The 64-bit timer time and base getters returned the error code for an
unknown timer, which a game could take for a time. They now return all
ones.

* kernel: report unknown UIDs with the right errors

Getting the class of a UID that isn't a thread manager object now fails
with INVALID_UID instead of UNKNOWN_UID, and deleting a thread that
doesn't exist fails with UNKNOWN_THREAD_ID instead of NOT_DORMANT.

* kernel: copy as much of an object's info as the game asks for

The info getters of callbacks, condition variables, event flags,
mutexes, lightweight mutexes and semaphores now copy the start of the
info struct, as many bytes as the size the game sets in it, and fail
with NO_MEMORY for a size past the struct. They used to fail for any
size but the struct's, or copy the whole struct for a larger one.

(cherry picked from commit 69cbf54290232e6df54ef912aa225b75934a1707)
